{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md | sed -n '65,215p'; cat collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj\"","aggregated_output":"    65\tcounterexample: `'a` bound to `#{X Y}` with equal bodies and `'b` bound\n    66\tto `X`; `'b` numbers `X`, after which `#{X Y}` is no longer tied), so tie\n    67\tgroups are deferred and re-examined to a fixpoint (D3). No label ever\n    68\tgrows: the encoding of an element is linear in the element.\n    69\t\n    70\t**F4. The residual tie class and the orbit rule.** What survives the\n    71\tfixpoint is a tie group whose unnumbered cursor cells have equal bodies\n    72\tand occur only inside tie groups. Two cases:\n    73\t- *Orbit case*: every such cursor cell occurs only inside one element of\n    74\t  the group. Then the elements are interchangeable and the group may be\n    75\t  entered in any order: the emitted rows do not depend on the order\n    76\t  (§3, lemma). `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` and `{X 1, Y 1}` are this case.\n    77\t- *Residue*: a cursor cell of the group occurs in two elements, or\n    78\t  twice across groups. Examples: `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}` (the 6a pinned\n    79\t  loop plus 2-cycle, `rows_test.cljc:312-329`) and the seven-cell\n    80\t  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`). Here the choice of\n    81\t  which element to enter first can change the rows (a triangle vertex\n    82\t  numbered 1 versus a square vertex), so discovery order alone cannot\n    83\t  decide. The honest answer is a refusal (D5): `:yin.k/non-portable`\n    84\t  kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` at lift, naming the container's path\n    85\t  and the cursor cells. Such a state needs two or more cursor cells on\n    86\t  one stream at one position held only through symmetric guest\n    87\t  collections of pairs; 6a refuses the seven-cell state too, by its\n    88\t  label-byte budget (`rows_test.cljc:400-404`).\n    89\t\n    90\t**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: a function of structure\n    91\talone, renaming-invariant, invariant under every permutation of\n    92\tunordered containers, terminating in at most `n` rounds of linear work.\n    93\t\n    94\t**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** Over 400 random states (one to five\n    95\tcursor cells, bodies drawn from four values so equal bodies are common,\n    96\tnested vectors, sets and maps under keyed bindings and the result slot)\n    97\tthe prototype gave equal root ids after renaming every cursor cell and\n    98\tshuffling every set and map, with no failure; it refused one state, the\n    99\t6a procedure two. The pinned two-cell fixture root\n   100\t`fab223…` and the ten-cell census root are unchanged; the review\n   101\tcounterexample and the alias fixtures receive new, hand-derived ids.\n   102\t\n   103\t**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n   104\t\n   105\t## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n   106\t\n   107\tInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\n   108\tcells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\n   109\ttask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\n   110\trefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\n   111\trule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n   112\t\n   113\t**Body label.** `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, the\n   114\tcursor cell's census body as plain data.\n   115\t\n   116\t**Content encoding `CE(x)`** under the numbering assigned so far:\n   117\tcarrier §6 step 2 with one change. An identity position encodes a\n   118\tnumbered cursor cell as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]` and an unnumbered one as\n   119\t`[:cursor-cell <body label>]`; nothing else changes: `[:lit d]` as\n   120\titself, `[:row B]` expanded in place, structural data as itself, the\n   121\tcensus as `[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]`, and the\n   122\telements of `:set` rows and the `[key value]` pairs of `:map`, `:store`,\n   123\t`:bindings` and `:parked-table` rows sorted bytewise, duplicates\n   124\tretained. `CE` is hash-free, label-free and linear in its argument.\n   125\t\n   126\t**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the root `:task` row.\n   127\tA row's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n   128\t(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\n   129\tkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, `:window`, the root's\n   130\t`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\n   131\tkey, the order §5 emits. The census is not entered; an install child's\n   132\t`:task` row is not entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries\n   133\t(each entry the pair `[key value]`) are sorted bytewise by `CE` under\n   134\tthe numbering at the moment the container is entered; a run of equal\n   135\tencodings is a **tie group** and is deferred; the remaining elements are\n   136\tentered in sorted order, an entry's key before its value.\n   137\t\n   138\t**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n   139\t`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\n   140\tcell has no number, the cursor cell receives `1 + (count of numbered\n   141\tcursor cells)`. An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\n   142\tno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n   143\t`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\n   144\tcontents table, so content rows keep sharing by Merkle id whether or\n   145\tnot they hold a cursor cell's first occurrence.\n   146\t\n   147\t**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the deferred tie groups are\n   148\tre-examined in deferral order: each is re-sorted by `CE` under the\n   149\tcurrent numbering; elements now alone in their encoding class are\n   150\tentered (D1, D2 apply inside them); equal runs are deferred again.\n   151\tRepeat while a round numbers at least one cursor cell.\n   152\t\n   153\t**D4. Orbit rule.** For each surviving tie group let `U` be the\n   154\tunnumbered cursor cells occurring in its elements. If every cursor cell\n   155\tof `U` has all its occurrences in the task inside one element of this\n   156\tgroup, enter the elements in the container's listed order. (By §3's\n   157\tlemma the emitted rows are the same for every order.)\n   158\t\n   159\t**D5. Refusal.** Otherwise the lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n   160\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the container's row path, and\n   161\ta `:yin.k/cursor-cells` list of the undecided cursor cells. A receiver\n   162\tthat reaches D5 while recomputing refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n   163\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is a resource refusal and neither\n   164\tis `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n   165\t\n   166\t**Validation** is unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs D1-D5,\n   167\tre-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells` on\n   168\tmismatch). The work bound (carrier §10.10) keeps rows, cursor cells,\n   169\tdepth and work; the search and label-byte bounds disappear.\n   170\t\n   171\t## 3. Proof of canonicality\n   172\t\n   173\tLet `S` be an abstract task and `ρ` a renaming of its cursor cells.\n   174\t\n   175\t1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant.* By induction over the\n   176\t   procedure's steps: each step reads only structural data, body labels\n   177\t   and numbers assigned by earlier steps; no step reads a source id. So\n   178\t   the run on `ρ(S)` makes the same decisions as the run on `S` and\n   179\t   assigns `N∘ρ⁻¹`; the emitted rows, which spell cursor cells by number\n   180\t   only, are byte-identical.\n   181\t2. *Invariant under permutation of unordered containers.* A `:set` or\n   182\t   `:map` is read in `CE` order, never in listed order (D1, D3), except\n   183\t   in D4, where the lemma applies. **Lemma (orbit).** Let a surviving tie\n   184\t   group have elements `e₁ … eₘ` with equal `CE`, and let every cursor\n   185\t   cell of `U` occur only inside one `eᵢ`. Equal `CE` means the elements\n   186\t   are identical once `U` is erased to body labels, with equal bodies at\n   187\t   corresponding places. Entering `e_{σ(1)} … e_{σ(m)}` assigns the\n   188\t   `j`-th block of fresh numbers to `e_{σ(j)}` in the same internal\n   189\t   pattern for every `j`; the emitted container is the same multiset of\n   190\t   elements for every `σ`, the census pairs the same numbers with equal\n   191\t   bodies, and no other row mentions a cursor cell of `U`. ∎\n   192\t3. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8\n   193\t   step 3), so D1 is finite; D3 runs at most `n` rounds because each\n   194\t   round numbers a cursor cell or stops; each round is linear in the\n   195\t   number of positions times the cost of `CE`, which is linear in the\n   196\t   element. No label grows.\n   197\t4. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n   198\t   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n   199\t5. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n   200\t   cells (D2 never renumbers), every occurrence of one cursor cell spells\n   201\t   one number, and two cursor cells at one position get two numbers.\n   202\t6. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n   203\t   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n   204\t   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by 1, equal rows.\n   205\t\n   206\tWhat is given up: the procedure does not number every state. D5 names\n   207\tthe refused class; a later search over that residue would not change\n   208\tthe numbering of any state D1-D4 number today, so the refusal can be\n   209\tnarrowed without churning ids.\n   210\t\n   211\t## 4. Amendments\n   212\t\n   213\t**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\n   214\twith §2 above (the body label, `CE`, D1-D5). Keep the section's first\n   215\tparagraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is defined, and what is\n(ns probe.discovery\n  \"Read-only prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering over the\n   6a abstract task representation (yin.vm.ucf.rows). Not repo code.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]))\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- helpers\n\n(defn descriptors [kind slots]\n  (let [schema (get rows/row-table kind)]\n    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\n          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n          :else schema)))\n\n(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\n\n(defn bytes-key [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\n\n(defn compare-bytes [a b]\n  (loop [i 0]\n    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n          (= i (count b)) 1\n          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n\n(defn by-bytes [xs] (vec (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key %1) (bytes-key %2)) xs)))\n\n(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table :window})\n\n;; -------------------------------------------------------- discovery order\n\n(defn discovery-numbering\n  \"Returns {:numbering {src -> :yin.k/cc-n}} or {:refused {...}}.\n   D1 traversal, D2 first meeting, D3 deferred fixpoint, D4 orbit rule,\n   D5 refusal.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [census (nth (:slots task) 9)\n        bodies (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n        label (fn [c] (let [[stream position] (:slots (get bodies c))]\n                        [:body (into [:stream] (:slots stream)) position]))\n        numbering (atom {})\n        deferred (atom [])                     ; vector of {:kind k :elems [...]}\n        occurrences (atom {})                  ; src -> count, census excluded\n        cell-ce (fn [c] (if-let [n (get @numbering c)] [:cursor-cell [:n n]] [:cursor-cell (label c)]))]\n    (letfn [(ce-slot [d x]\n              (cond (ref? d) (ce x)\n                    (refs? d) (mapv ce x)\n                    (= :C d) (cell-ce x)\n                    (v? d) (case (first x)\n                             :cursor-cell (cell-ce (second x))\n                             :row [:row (ce (second x))]\n                             x)\n                    :else x))\n            (ce [n]\n              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n                (cond (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\n                      (and (= :task kind) (not (identical? n task))) [:child]\n                      :else (let [ss (mapv ce-slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)\n                                  ss (cond (contains? #{:map :store :bindings :parked-table} kind)\n                                           (by-bytes (mapv vec (partition 2 ss)))\n                                           (= :set kind) (by-bytes ss)\n                                           :else ss)]\n                              (into [kind] ss)))))\n            (count-occ-slot [d x]\n              (cond (ref? d) (count-occ x)\n                    (refs? d) (run! count-occ x)\n                    (= :C d) (swap! occurrences update x (fnil inc 0))\n                    (v? d) (case (first x)\n                             :cursor-cell (swap! occurrences update (second x) (fnil inc 0))\n                             :row (count-occ (second x))\n                             nil)\n                    :else nil))\n            (count-occ [n]\n              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n                (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells kind) (and (= :task kind) (not (identical? n task))))\n                  (dorun (map count-occ-slot (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))\n            (meet [c]\n              (when-not (contains? @numbering c)\n                (swap! numbering assoc c (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"cc-\" (inc (count @numbering)))))))\n            (visit-slot [d x]\n              (cond (ref? d) (visit x)\n                    (refs? d) (run! visit x)\n                    (= :C d) (meet x)\n                    (v? d) (case (first x)\n                             :cursor-cell (meet (second x))\n                             :row (visit (second x))\n                             nil)\n                    :else nil))\n            (visit-unordered [kind elems]\n              ;; elems: for :set the V elements; for :map the [k v] pairs\n              (let [keyed (mapv (fn [e] [(bytes-key (if (= :map kind) [(ce-slot :V (first e)) (ce-slot :V (second e))] (ce-slot :V e))) e]) elems)\n                    sorted (sort #(compare-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) keyed)\n                    groups (partition-by first sorted)]\n                (doseq [g groups]\n                  (if (= 1 (count g))\n                    (visit-elem kind (second (first g)))\n                    (swap! deferred conj {:kind kind :elems (mapv second g)})))))\n            (visit-elem [kind e]\n              (if (= :map kind)\n                (do (visit-slot :V (first e)) (visit-slot :V (second e)))\n                (visit-slot :V e)))\n            (visit [n]\n              (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n                (cond\n                  (= :cursor-cells kind) nil\n                  (and (= :task kind) (not (identical? n task))) nil   ; child: own scope\n                  (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n                  (doseq [[_ v] (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (first %1)) (bytes-key (first %2))) (partition 2 slots))]\n                    (visit-slot :V v))\n                  (= :map kind) (visit-unordered :map (mapv vec (partition 2 slots)))\n                  (= :set kind) (visit-unordered :set (vec slots))\n                  (= :task kind)\n                  (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\n                    (cond (= i 7) (doseq [ms (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (first (:slots %1))) (bytes-key (first (:slots %2)))) x)] (visit ms))\n                          (= i 12) (doseq [ins (sort #(compare-bytes (bytes-key (first (:slots %1))) (bytes-key (first (:slots %2)))) x)] (visit ins))\n                          :else (visit-slot d x)))\n                  :else (dorun (map visit-slot ds slots)))))\n            (fixpoint []\n              (loop []\n                (let [groups @deferred\n                      before (count @numbering)]\n                  (reset! deferred [])\n                  (doseq [{:keys [kind elems]} groups] (visit-unordered kind elems))\n                  (when (and (seq @deferred) (> (count @numbering) before)) (recur)))))\n            (unnumbered-cells [kind e]\n              (let [acc (atom [])]\n                (letfn [(walk-slot [d x]\n                          (cond (ref? d) (walk x)\n                                (= :C d) (when-not (get @numbering x) (swap! acc conj x))\n                                (v? d) (case (first x)\n                                         :cursor-cell (when-not (get @numbering (second x)) (swap! acc conj (second x)))\n                                         :row (walk (second x))\n                                         nil)\n                                :else nil))\n                        (walk [n] (dorun (map walk-slot (descriptors (:kind n) (:slots n)) (:slots n))))]\n                  (if (= :map kind) (do (walk-slot :V (first e)) (walk-slot :V (second e))) (walk-slot :V e))\n                  @acc)))]\n      (count-occ task)\n      (visit task)\n      (fixpoint)\n      ;; D4 orbit rule / D5 refusal on the residue\n      (let [residue @deferred\n            ;; General orbit rule: every unnumbered cell of the group has ALL its\n            ;; occurrences in the task inside one element of this group.\n            bad (some (fn [{:keys [kind elems]}]\n                        (let [per-elem (mapv #(frequencies (unnumbered-cells kind %)) elems)\n                              cells (distinct (mapcat keys per-elem))]\n                          (when (some (fn [c]\n                                        (let [in (filter #(contains? % c) per-elem)]\n                                          (or (not= 1 (count in))\n                                              (not= (get (first in) c) (get @occurrences c)))))\n                                      cells)\n                            {:kind :unresolved-cursor-cells :container kind :cells (vec cells)})))\n                      residue)]\n        (if bad\n          {:refused bad}\n          (do (doseq [{:keys [kind elems]} residue] (doseq [e elems] (visit-elem kind e)))\n              {:numbering @numbering}))))))\n\n(defn do-root [task]\n  (let [r (discovery-numbering task)]\n    (if (:refused r) r (:yin.k/root (rows/emit-numbered task (:numbering r))))))\n\n(defn safe-6a-root [task]\n  (try (:yin.k/root (rows/emit task))\n       (catch Throwable e {:refused (select-keys (ex-data e) [:yin.k/kind :yin.k/path])})))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ generators\n\n(defn rename-and-shuffle\n  \"Rename every cursor cell by `bij` and shuffle every :set and :map in source order.\"\n  [n bij rnd]\n  (letfn [(value [v] (case (first v)\n                       :cursor-cell [:cursor-cell (bij (second v))]\n                       :row [:row (go (second v))]\n                       v))\n          (go [n]\n            (let [{:keys [kind slots]} n]\n              (case kind\n                :set (rows/node :set (vec (shuffle (mapv value slots))))   ; shuffle uses global rnd; seeded below\n                :map (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity (shuffle (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(value k) (value v)]) (partition 2 slots))))))\n                :cursor-cells (rows/node :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat (fn [[c b]] [(bij c) (go b)]) (shuffle (mapv vec (partition 2 slots))))))\n                (rows/node kind (mapv (fn [d x]\n                                        (cond (ref? d) (go x)\n                                              (refs? d) (mapv go x)\n                                              (= :C d) (bij x)\n                                              (v? d) (if x (value x) x)\n                                              :else x))\n                                      (descriptors kind slots) slots)))))]\n    (go n)))\n\n(defn gen-value\n  \"Random V over cells; depth-bounded. Returns V.\"\n  [^java.util.Random r cells depth]\n  (let [pick (fn [xs] (nth xs (.nextInt r (count xs))))\n        k (.nextInt r (if (pos? depth) 6 3))]\n    (case k\n      0 [:lit (pick [1 2 :k \"s\" nil])]\n      1 [:cursor-cell (pick cells)]\n      2 [:cursor-cell (pick cells)]\n      3 [:row (rows/node :vec (vec (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))]\n      4 (let [elems (vec (distinct (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 4)) #(gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))]\n          (if (some #(not= :lit (first %)) elems) [:row (rows/node :set elems)] [:lit (set (map second elems))]))\n      5 (let [pairs (vec (vals (into {} (map (fn [p] [(pr-str (first p)) p]))\n                                     (repeatedly (inc (.nextInt r 3)) #(vector (gen-value r cells (dec depth)) (gen-value r cells (dec depth)))))))]\n          (if (some (fn [[a b]] (or (not= :lit (first a)) (not= :lit (first b)))) pairs)\n            [:row (rows/node :map (vec (mapcat identity pairs)))]\n            [:lit (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(second a) (second b)])) pairs)])))))\n\n(defn reached [v]\n  (let [acc (atom #{})]\n    (letfn [(w [v] (case (first v)\n                     :cursor-cell (swap! acc conj (second v))\n                     :row (doseq [s (:slots (second v))] (w s))\n                     nil))]\n      (w v) @acc)))\n\n(defn gen-task\n  [seed]\n  (let [r (java.util.Random. seed)\n        k (inc (.nextInt r 5))\n        cells (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"c\" %)) (range k))\n        bodies (mapv (fn [_] (rt/cursor-cell-body (nth [:s :t] (.nextInt r 2)) (.nextInt r 2))) cells)\n        names (vec (take (inc (.nextInt r 3)) '[a b c]))\n        vals (mapv (fn [_] (gen-value r cells 3)) names)\n        result (gen-value r cells 2)\n        used (reduce into (reached result) (map reached vals))\n        census (keep (fn [[c b]] (when (used c) [c b])) (map vector cells bodies))]\n    (if (empty? used)\n      (recur (inc seed))\n      (let [t (rt/task-node census result)]\n        (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store (vec (mapcat (fn [n v] [n v]) names vals)))))))))\n\n(defn fixture-review-counterexample\n  \"'a bound to #{X Y} with equal bodies, 'b bound to X.\"\n  []\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        t (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:lit nil])]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots t) 6 (rows/node :store ['a [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]\n                                                             'b [:cursor-cell :x]])))))\n\n(defn report-fixture [name task]\n  (let [d (discovery-numbering task)]\n    (println name)\n    (println \"  DO :\" (or (:refused d) (into (sorted-map) (:numbering d))))\n    (println \"  DO root:\" (do-root task))\n    (println \"  6a root:\" (safe-6a-root task))))\n\n(defn -main []\n  (println \"== fixtures\")\n  (report-fixture \"fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y (pinned fab223...)\" (rt/fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y))\n  (report-fixture \"review counterexample 'a #{X Y}, 'b X\" (fixture-review-counterexample))\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)]\n    (report-fixture \"symmetric pair #{X Y}\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]))\n    (report-fixture \"alias a: [x x y]\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])]))\n    (report-fixture \"alias b: [x y y]\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y] [:cursor-cell :y]])]))\n    (let [shape (fn [[l a b]] [[:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell l] [:cursor-cell l]])]\n                               [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell a] [:cursor-cell b]])]\n                               [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell b] [:cursor-cell a]])]])\n          graph (fn [ids edges] (rt/task-node (mapv #(vector % body) ids) [:row (rows/node :set edges)]))]\n      (report-fixture \"loop + 2-cycle (pinned 55ff16...)\" (graph [:l :a :b] (shape [:l :a :b])))))\n  (report-fixture \"seven-cell triangle+square\" (rt/seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square))\n  (println \"\\n== orbit rule, element reversal + renaming\")\n  (let [body (rt/cursor-cell-body :s 0)\n        orbit [[\"#{[X X] [Y Y]}\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]]\n                                               [:row (rows/node :set [[:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :x]])]\n                                                                      [:row (rows/node :vec [[:cursor-cell :y] [:cursor-cell :y]])]])])]\n               [\"{X 1, Y 1}\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]]\n                                           [:row (rows/node :map [[:cursor-cell :x] [:lit 1] [:cursor-cell :y] [:lit 1]])])]\n               [\"#{X Y}\" (rt/task-node [[:x body] [:y body]] [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell :x] [:cursor-cell :y]])])]\n               [\"review counterexample\" (fixture-review-counterexample)]]]\n    (doseq [[name task] orbit]\n      (let [rev (with-redefs [shuffle (comp vec reverse)] (rename-and-shuffle task identity nil)) ren (rename-and-shuffle task {:x :q/y :y :q/x} nil)]\n        (println name \"DO:\" (do-root task) \"| reversed equal:\" (= (do-root task) (do-root rev))\n                 \"| renamed+shuffled equal:\" (= (do-root task) (do-root ren))))))\n  (let [ids (mapv #(keyword \"src\" (str \"x\" %)) (range 10))]\n    (report-fixture \"ten cells in a vec\" (rt/task-node (mapv (fn [id i] [id (rt/cursor-cell-body :s i)]) ids (range))\n                                                       [:row (rows/node :vec (mapv #(vector :cursor-cell %) ids))])))\n  (println \"\\n== counterexample search\")\n  (let [n 400\n        stats (atom {:do-ok 0 :do-refused 0 :sixa-refused 0 :same-root 0 :diff-root 0 :do-invariance-fail 0 :sixa-invariance-fail 0})]\n    (doseq [seed (range n)]\n      (let [task (gen-task seed)\n            cells (vec (take-nth 2 (:slots (nth (:slots task) 9))))\n            fresh (zipmap cells (shuffle (mapv #(keyword \"other\" (str \"z\" %)) (range (count cells)))))\n            task2 (rename-and-shuffle task fresh nil)\n            d1 (do-root task) d2 (do-root task2)]\n        (if (map? d1)\n          (swap! stats update :do-refused inc)\n          (do (swap! stats update :do-ok inc)\n              (when (not= d1 d2) (swap! stats update :do-invariance-fail inc)\n                    (println \"DO INVARIANCE FAIL seed\" seed))))\n        (let [s1 (safe-6a-root task) s2 (safe-6a-root task2)]\n          (cond (map? s1) (swap! stats update :sixa-refused inc)\n                (not= s1 s2) (do (swap! stats update :sixa-invariance-fail inc) (println \"6a INVARIANCE FAIL seed\" seed))\n                :else (if (= s1 d1) (swap! stats update :same-root inc) (swap! stats update :diff-root inc))))))\n    (println n \"random states:\" @stats)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,270p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; cat collab/numbering-probe/deps.edn; command -v git; command -v clojure\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,270p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; cat collab/numbering-probe/deps.edn; command -v git; command -v clojure\"","aggregated_output":"# UCF transported state as tuples: the row carrier (body version 3)\n\nStatus: **Draft, proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-10 from the owner\ndiscussion recorded in\n`public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog`\nPart Three; revised the same day after the codex Architect review\n`collab/1791500000000-architect-ucf-transport-tuples-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(round 1: thirteen findings; round 2: eight; round 3: seven; all\naddressed below, and nine answers adopted in §10). §10 also lists which\nparts of this document are design proposals rather than consequences of\nthe owner's rulings.\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), to UCF §7 in\n[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md),\nand to the version-2 amendment\n[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md),\nwhose body is the fact list this carrier must reproduce. Follows the row\nconventions of [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md).\nNothing here is implemented. This document specifies body version 3, the\none carrier; older bodies are not read.\n\n> **Amendment 2026-10-11.** This draft is amended by\n> `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §10.3 (R1-R11, codex\n> rulings of 2026-10-10) for the register-shaped canonical machine of\n> `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`, and the amended text below\n> **supersedes** the draft's earlier §5.2 (the `:regs`/`:stack`/\n> `stack-base` frame rows), its §8 step 7 (stack-base checks) and its\n> \"the `\"v3\"` stamp does not move\" positioning: the carrier is body\n> version 3 with one engine-less profile\n> `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`, reads no other body\n> version, carries canonical vectors by A, `:yin.k/trees` and the task's\n> free environment, never a native image, and its frames are `:act` and\n> `:tail` rows judged by the frame validator. Amended: the status\n> paragraph (R1a), §2 (R1, R7), §5.1 `:reified` (R3), §5.2 (R2), §5.4\n> `:parked-table` (R4), §5.5 (R5, R10), §8 steps 1 and 7 (R6a, R6), §9\n> (R8), §10.3-10.8 (R7, R8, R9), §11 (R9); §3 kept verbatim (R11).\n> Completed against continuation §3.3 and §7.1 on review (codex,\n> 2026-10-11): §8 step 7 runs `frame/task-defect` once per body before\n> its frames; §8 step 9's census roots and §9's lift walk include the\n> initial free environment; §5.2 cites rule A2.\n\n## 1. Stance\n\nThree owner rulings fix the shape of this document.\n\n1. *Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints.* The Universal AST is\n   the canonical code; the instruction vector is its deterministic\n   lowering; a safepoint is `(segment-address, pc)` into that vector\n   (UCF §7.3.2, §7.4.1). This carrier changes nothing about code\n   identity, safepoints, or execution profiles.\n2. *The transported state is a tuple set.* Sharing and the literal/marker\n   distinction stop being special cases: a reference is a row id in a\n   typed position, sharing is Merkle, and a program's data is wrapped\n   where it can be confused with a reference. What does not collapse is\n   the per-entity decision between content and identity, which this\n   carrier makes a property of the row kind and then commits to the root\n   through a census.\n3. *Tuples are the transport form only.* They exist between a lift and a\n   lower. At rest they are rows like any other and queryable by the\n   ordinary interpreter. Nothing executes them; the destination lowers\n   them into its native representation.\n\nThis proposal chooses positional rows: a row kind fixes a slot list, one\ntable serves encoder and decoder, and a row's Merkle id commits to its\nchildren inline, as code rows already do. EAV is expressive enough and\nnothing in the invariants rules it out; positional rows are chosen for\ncompactness, closedness, and consistency with the existing row\nconventions, not because EAV was rejected by review.\n\n## 2. Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n\n**Body version 3, one profile.** The carrier is body version 3 and reads\nno other version (owner, 2026-10-11: no legacy): a body of version 0, 1\nor 2 is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` at the gate and is never normalised or\nupgraded. The profile map is `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`;\nthere is no engine key, because the frame is the canonical\nregister-shaped machine's and the executing kernel is the receiving\ncomposition's choice: the stack and register kernels lower the same rows\nthrough their layouts (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n§2.1, §4). Any other profile map is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n\n## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n\nEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\nas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n\n**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\nsomething to run on before any row is interpreted:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true\n :yin.k/version 3\n :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n```\n\nUnlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\nany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so every walk is over a map from\nid to one row. The gate's walk over install children follows only\n`:install` child crefs and keeps an **active recursion-path set**: a\ncref that leaves the rows, or a child that is already on the active\npath, stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable`. A child reached again\n*off* the active path is not an error: two install entries whose\nchildren have identical content (two identical halted children, say)\nshare one child row by Merkle construction, the structural graph is a\nDAG, and each install entry still restores as its own native task\ninstance with its own task-local cursor cells and its own contextual custody\nvalidation. Completed content validation is cached per row; instance\nrestoration never is. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what\nis hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in\n`:rows` is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`: **the same\ncall, and therefore the same digest, as code addresses `A`**\n(`ucf/code-address` is `(jing/segment-key v)`), which is Jing's default\nalgorithm, BLAKE3 at the time of writing (`dao.jing.cbor.md`,\n`dao.jing.hash-registry.md`). The carrier mints no second addressing\nscheme beside A. Uniqueness, which a canonical address scheme needs, is\nsupplied by the **carrier version**, not by naming an algorithm here:\n**body version 3 uses BLAKE3 permanently**; this is an immutable\nmapping from carrier version to digest, never the reader's current\ndefault, and a later Jing default requires another carrier version,\nexactly as it would for A. Version-3 verification and re-emission (§6,\n§8) always use BLAKE3, whatever the reader's default is at the time;\nthis matters because root body bytes contain child-row addresses, so\nthe numbering procedure's leaf keys depend on the digest and are\ncanonical *within* a carrier version. Addresses are self-describing\n(`:segment/blake3-…`, `:segment/sha256-…`); a reader verifies each id\nunder the algorithm its address names, as Jing always does, and refuses\nan id whose algorithm is not the one version 3 mints under\n(`:yin.k/undecodable`). Equal bodies are one row. The id is Merkle: it\ncommits to every row id in the body.\n\n**Identity rows** are cursor cells (UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor,\ncursor cell, cursor-ref, cell\", which defines all four words; this document\nuses them exactly as defined there). Before 2026-10-12 this document called\nthe cursor cell a \"cell\" and spelled its tags `:cells`, `:cell-body`,\n`[:cell C]`, `:yin.k/c-<n>`; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps those\nspellings. A cursor cell has a task-local id `C`\nin the form `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\nnumbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\ncarries it: a cursor-cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\nquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/cc-1` are unrelated.\n\n**The census.** A cursor cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\nare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\ncursor cell commits to the cursor cell's identity but not to what the cursor cell holds. The\nroot therefore carries a **cursor-cell census**, a content row\n\n```clojure\n[X :cursor-cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n[Bᵢ :cursor-cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n```\n\nthat maps every cursor-cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\nslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cursor cell's contents. A\nchange to any cursor cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\nthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\nauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\nlist exactly the cursor cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\nroot and per install child.\n\n**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n\n- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cursor-cell iref in a value, a\n  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n  and a `:cursor-cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n  (data), so no cursor cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n  validates, it does not trust construction.\n- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n\n## 4. The value union\n\nEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\na two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n\n| `V` | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n| `[:cursor-cell C]` | a reference to the identity cursor cell `C` |\n\nThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\nliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\ncan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\nis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n\n**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\narms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\nRational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\nsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\nvalues and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\nretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\nare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\nkind `:byte-string`.\n\n**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\nportability and whether a value reaches a reference. Reference-free\nretained metadata on a *literal* travels as Jing preserves it and is\nhashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\n**any collection that must become a row and carries retained metadata\nrefuses**, whether the metadata holds a reference (copying it as data\nwould carry source coordinates and seals unremapped) or is ordinary\nreference-free data such as `{:doc \"x\"}`: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:metadata` naming the path. This is a proposed limitation of this\ncarrier, not an inherited UCF requirement (§10). Metadata a reader strips\nis not retained metadata and plays no part.\n\nA guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\ndata inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n\nA collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\nreference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\nholds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\nand **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\ncollections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\ndecision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\ncollection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n\n**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\nguest literals and contain no guest values, namely install responses,\nlink envelopes, operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data\nslot holds plain canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that\ngoverns it (an install response by the linker's delivery checks, an\nop-id by §7.4.3 r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these\nslots `D`. A retained **FFI request envelope is not structural data**:\n`dao.stream.apply/request?` permits unknown envelope keys, and its\narguments are guest values. It therefore travels as the existing\nencoder carries it (`handoff.cljc`): the **complete envelope,\nrecursively encoded as a `V`** (a `:map` row, or `[:lit m]` when it\nreaches no reference), so that every key is preserved, closures stay\nportable, and resource references go through the census. No closed\nenvelope row narrows the open request protocol.\n\nPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\nnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\ntheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n\n## 5. The row table\n\nKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\nnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\nnaming the row and slot. **One ordering rule** governs every emitted row\nand every receiver check: \"sorted\" means ordered by the Jing canonical\nbytes of the sort key *as spelled in the emitted row*, cursor-cell numbers\nincluded, under the final numbering of §6. The class-erased skeletons of\n§6 are used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the\norder of an emitted row. Ordered slots are part of the schema because\nthey determine the Merkle id.\n\n`N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\ncode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cursor-cell id,\n`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\nonly the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\n{:deps {datomworld/root {:local/root \"../..\"}}\n :paths [\"src\" \"../../test\"]}\n/usr/bin/git\n/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/clojure/1.12.4.1602/bin/clojure\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
